VALUES ('63368e78-2b0d-4abd-8a2f-6a1a58a1467e', 'aws-saa-practice-8', 'AWS Certified Solutions Architect - Associate Practice Paper 8', 'aws-saa', ARRAY['Parameter Store', 'S3 Access Points', 'Firewall Manager', 'IMDSv2', 'RDS Multi-AZ Cluster', 'Warm Pools', 'Adaptive Capacity', 'Capacity Reservations']::TEXT[], 20, 'standard', true, 8, 30);

-- ── Domain: Secure Architectures (30% — 6 questions) ──

VALUES ('8c50ddbc-ee33-4a06-8ab3-12685b634e11', '63368e78-2b0d-4abd-8a2f-6a1a58a1467e', 1, 'A company needs to store a large number of application configuration values — some sensitive (database connection strings), most not (feature flags, environment names) — and wants a lower-cost option than Secrets Manager for the non-sensitive majority, while still having somewhere to keep the sensitive ones with encryption and access control. What AWS service, distinct from Secrets Manager, is commonly used for this broader configuration-storage need?');
VALUES ('8c50ddbc-ee33-4a06-8ab3-12685b634e11', '2ea5bb17-57cb-44dc-b028-b1f4cef7f1ac', 'Parameter Store and Secrets Manager (covered generally in a prior paper) solve genuinely overlapping but distinctly-scoped problems: Secrets Manager is purpose-built specifically around SECRETS that benefit from automatic, built-in ROTATION (most commonly database credentials) and charges per stored secret, while Parameter Store''s Standard tier is free and designed for BROADER configuration storage — a mix of genuinely sensitive values (still KMS-encryptable) and the large volume of non-sensitive configuration (feature flags, environment names, non-secret settings) most applications also need to store somewhere centrally — without needing to pay Secrets Manager''s per-secret cost for values that don''t actually need its rotation-specific feature set. Parameter Store also supports hierarchical naming (organizing parameters into a path-like structure) for organizing large numbers of values cleanly. This is a genuinely common, practical cost-and-fit decision — choosing the right one (or using both together for their respective strengths) rather than defaulting to Secrets Manager for every configuration value regardless of whether its specific rotation capability is actually needed. AWS Config (covered in a prior paper) tracks resource CONFIGURATION STATE/compliance drift, an entirely different concept from application-level configuration value storage, and CloudTrail (covered in a prior paper) is an API-activity audit log, unrelated to either.');

VALUES ('18b94d7b-bcbf-4f3f-a7ee-6a13a836bcae', '63368e78-2b0d-4abd-8a2f-6a1a58a1467e', 2, 'A company wants an IAM policy that allows a specific, highly sensitive action (like deleting a production S3 bucket) ONLY when the calling principal has recently authenticated with a second MFA factor, in addition to whatever normal IAM permissions they otherwise hold — rejecting the action entirely if MFA wasn''t used for that session, even from an otherwise fully-permitted principal. What IAM policy condition key enforces this?');
VALUES ('18b94d7b-bcbf-4f3f-a7ee-6a13a836bcae', 'b2ce7b61-3d18-4b3f-93f1-dba57db76920', 'The `aws:MultiFactorAuthPresent` condition key directly extends the general IAM policy CONDITION concept (covered generally, via the source-IP restriction example, in a prior paper) to specifically gate a sensitive action on MFA having been used for the CURRENT session — meaning a principal with otherwise-broad, valid permissions still can''t perform the gated action unless they specifically authenticated with MFA for that session, adding a genuinely meaningful extra layer of protection for particularly destructive or sensitive actions beyond whatever baseline permissions a role or user otherwise carries, directly complementary in spirit to S3''s MFA Delete concept (covered in a prior paper, which requires MFA specifically for permanent version deletion) but applied generally to any IAM-governed action via policy, not limited to S3 versioning. This is a genuinely different condition key from `aws:SourceIp` (covered generally in a prior paper, restricting by network origin, not authentication factor) and from `aws:SecureTransport` (covered in a prior paper, requiring HTTPS specifically, not MFA) — three separate, real IAM condition keys addressing three separate, distinct restriction dimensions, and IAM genuinely does support this kind of granular, per-action MFA requirement via policy condition, not only account-level sign-in MFA.');

VALUES ('d194dc97-9b4a-41a8-89e1-34f05e3c4f86', '63368e78-2b0d-4abd-8a2f-6a1a58a1467e', 3, 'A company has a single S3 bucket shared by multiple internal teams, each of which should only be able to access a specific prefix (folder) within that bucket, and managing this through one increasingly complex bucket policy has become error-prone as more teams and access patterns are added. What S3 feature lets each team be given its own dedicated, simpler access configuration for its own portion of the shared bucket?');
VALUES ('d194dc97-9b4a-41a8-89e1-34f05e3c4f86', 'e2491e0d-a7c9-4c33-a8e6-be1c92009855', 'S3 Access Points specifically address the "one bucket policy trying to do too much" problem by letting access management be broken into multiple, smaller, independently-managed named endpoints, each with its OWN policy scoped to whatever subset of the bucket (a specific prefix, for instance) and whatever network restrictions (like requiring VPC-only access) are appropriate for that specific team or application — genuinely simplifying what would otherwise be one sprawling, hard-to-audit bucket policy trying to encode every team''s distinct access rules in a single document. This is meaningfully different from S3 Multi-Region Access Points (covered in a prior paper, which solve a completely different problem — routing to the lowest-latency copy of REPLICATED data across MULTIPLE REGIONS, not scoping access within a single-region bucket by prefix) — a genuinely common point of exam confusion given the similar "Access Point" naming, worth being precise about. S3 Access Points are a real, standard, purpose-built feature specifically for simplifying multi-tenant or multi-team bucket access management, not a redundant restatement of the standard bucket policy or the Multi-Region variant.');

VALUES ('a254f759-5dee-46c8-9ad8-d36eca0a3f3f', '63368e78-2b0d-4abd-8a2f-6a1a58a1467e', 4, 'A media company wants to serve premium video content through CloudFront ONLY to users who have a valid, paid subscription — generating a unique, time-limited URL (or cookie) for each authorized viewing session so that the content cannot simply be freely shared or accessed by anyone who happens to obtain the CloudFront distribution''s general URL. What CloudFront feature is purpose-built for this kind of restricted, per-session content access?');
VALUES ('a254f759-5dee-46c8-9ad8-d36eca0a3f3f', 'eb9e2bcb-815f-4677-bfd3-0690bfe3c4c3', 'CloudFront Signed URLs and Signed Cookies are specifically purpose-built for gating access to CloudFront-served content to only authorized, time-limited sessions — genuinely distinct from S3 pre-signed URLs (covered in a prior paper, which grant temporary access directly to an S3 OBJECT, bypassing CloudFront entirely) since Signed URLs/Cookies operate at the CDN layer itself, restricting what CloudFront will actually serve regardless of how a request arrives. Signed URLs are typically used for restricting access to a SINGLE file, while Signed Cookies are better suited to restricting access to MULTIPLE files at once (like every segment of a streaming video session) without needing to generate and distribute a separate signed URL for each individual segment — a genuinely useful distinction for exactly this premium-video-streaming use case. This directly addresses the "anyone with the general distribution URL could access this" problem a plain, unrestricted CloudFront distribution would otherwise have, making it a real, standard, and purpose-built access-control mechanism specifically for CDN-served content, not a capability CloudFront lacks.');

VALUES ('1afb9b13-fbd4-41b5-9f9f-3b29f4812b58', '63368e78-2b0d-4abd-8a2f-6a1a58a1467e', 5, 'A company with dozens of AWS accounts under AWS Organizations wants to enforce a consistent AWS WAF rule set, Shield Advanced protection, and security group policy across every account and every application resource — centrally managed from one place, rather than each individual account/team configuring (and potentially misconfiguring or forgetting) these security controls independently. What AWS service is purpose-built for this centralized, Organization-wide security policy management?');
VALUES ('1afb9b13-fbd4-41b5-9f9f-3b29f4812b58', 'b65a5194-5f63-4c77-974f-72a7d933ef03', 'AWS Firewall Manager is specifically the centralized POLICY ENFORCEMENT layer for exactly this need, genuinely distinct from Security Hub (covered in a prior paper, which AGGREGATES and correlates security FINDINGS from multiple sources like GuardDuty for VISIBILITY, but does not itself configure or enforce WAF/Shield/security-group policy) — Firewall Manager instead lets a central security team define policies ONCE (a required WAF rule set, mandatory Shield Advanced protection, a baseline security group configuration) and have those policies automatically applied and enforced consistently across every account and resource in the Organization, critically including NEW resources and accounts as they''re created going forward, removing the real risk of an individual team configuring these protections inconsistently, incorrectly, or simply forgetting to configure them at all. This directly complements SCPs (covered in a prior paper, which set IAM permission BOUNDARIES organization-wide) and IAM Access Analyzer (covered in a prior paper, which DETECTS unintended exposure) — Firewall Manager''s specific role is proactively ENFORCING a consistent security POLICY baseline for these particular network/application-layer protections specifically, a real, purpose-built, and directly relevant AWS Organizations-integrated service.');

VALUES ('d385f689-c8bb-499c-b925-49e26e1d30e5', '63368e78-2b0d-4abd-8a2f-6a1a58a1467e', 6, 'A security audit discovers that an EC2 instance''s Instance Metadata Service (IMDS) — used to retrieve the instance''s attached IAM role credentials, among other instance data — is configured in a way that could allow a Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF) vulnerability in a hosted web application to trick the instance into retrieving and leaking those IAM credentials to an external attacker. What EC2 configuration change specifically hardens IMDS against exactly this SSRF-based credential-theft technique?');
VALUES ('d385f689-c8bb-499c-b925-49e26e1d30e5', 'ad21c341-22a5-4ebf-b523-1b33380e6b91', 'This is a real, well-documented, and genuinely significant hardening step directly connecting to how an EC2 instance role (covered generally in a prior paper, as the AWS-recommended way to grant an application AWS API access without hardcoded credentials) actually retrieves its temporary credentials — under IMDSv1, retrieving those credentials was a single, simple, UNAUTHENTICATED GET request to a well-known local address, meaning a web application vulnerability that lets an attacker trigger arbitrary outbound requests from the server (an SSRF vulnerability, connecting to the general web-application-vulnerability theme covered in Security+ content) could be tricked into making that same simple GET request on the attacker''s behalf and returning the instance''s live IAM credentials in the response — a real, demonstrated, and historically significant attack pattern in actual incidents. IMDSv2 closes this specific gap by requiring an initial, session-establishing PUT request (to obtain a token) before any metadata GET request will be honored — a PUT-based, header-requiring interaction that a typical simple SSRF vulnerability (which usually can only induce basic GET-style requests) generally cannot replicate, meaningfully raising the bar against this specific attack technique. EBS optimization (covered in a prior paper, for storage-traffic bandwidth) and Placement Groups (covered in a prior paper, for inter-instance network placement) are both entirely unrelated to IMDS credential-retrieval security — this is a real, documented AWS security best practice specifically addressing a real, previously-exploited vulnerability class, not a non-issue.');

-- ── Domain: Resilient Architectures (26% — 5 questions) ──

VALUES ('b855fbea-f171-4eb2-be37-156cff0b4adb', '63368e78-2b0d-4abd-8a2f-6a1a58a1467e', 7, 'A company wants to replicate specific S3 data to a bucket owned by a SEPARATE AWS account entirely (not just a different bucket or region within the same account) — for example, sending a compliance copy of data to an auditor''s own AWS account, or isolating a backup copy in a dedicated, separately-controlled account for additional protection against a compromise of the primary account. Does S3 replication support this cross-account destination, and what additional configuration does it require compared to same-account replication?');
VALUES ('b855fbea-f171-4eb2-be37-156cff0b4adb', '9da2c97f-e0b3-4eb3-b5d6-01ed810a9c02', 'S3 replication genuinely supports cross-account destinations, directly building on the Same-Region and Cross-Region Replication concepts covered generally in prior papers, extending the same underlying replication mechanism (requiring versioning on both buckets) across an account boundary rather than only within one account — a genuinely valuable pattern for exactly the scenarios described: isolating backup/compliance copies in a separately-controlled account specifically so a compromise of the PRIMARY account''s credentials doesn''t automatically also compromise the backup copy sitting in that same account, or sharing a compliance copy directly with an external party''s own AWS account. The real, required additional step compared to same-account replication is EXPLICIT PERMISSION from the destination account''s owner — since a source account cannot unilaterally write objects into a bucket it doesn''t own, the destination bucket''s policy must specifically authorize the source account''s replication role, and object OWNERSHIP is typically configured so the replicated copy is genuinely owned by the destination account (not left owned by the source account sitting in someone else''s bucket, which would complicate the destination account''s own management of it). This is a real, standard, well-documented S3 capability, not a technical impossibility, and the destination account absolutely retains meaningful control via its own bucket policy — it isn''t forced to accept replication with no say in the matter.');

VALUES ('d0eee210-0591-4301-b011-3415cdd40584', '63368e78-2b0d-4abd-8a2f-6a1a58a1467e', 8, 'A company running RDS wants FASTER failover than classic Multi-AZ provides (which promotes a single standby), and specifically wants the ability to also offload READ traffic to a standby instance during normal operation rather than that standby sitting completely idle except during an actual failover. What newer RDS deployment option provides both faster failover AND a readable standby?');
VALUES ('d0eee210-0591-4301-b011-3415cdd40584', '2a0e55cd-dadf-4fba-b39b-c4dbccd44b1d', 'RDS Multi-AZ DB Cluster deployment is a genuinely newer, distinct option from classic (single-standby) Multi-AZ (covered generally, including its automatic-failover mechanism, in a prior paper) — rather than one standby that sits completely idle and unreadable until an actual failover event, a Multi-AZ DB Cluster maintains TWO readable standby instances using a quorum-based replication protocol, which both meaningfully speeds up failover (typically achieving sub-minute recovery, faster than classic Multi-AZ''s roughly one-to-two-minute failover) AND lets an application offload read traffic to those standbys during completely normal operation, rather than that standby capacity going to waste outside of a failure scenario. This is a genuinely different, newer deployment option from a standard RDS Read Replica (covered generally in a prior paper, which uses ASYNCHRONOUS replication and requires MANUAL promotion, not automatic quorum-based failover) — Multi-AZ DB Cluster specifically combines the AUTOMATIC failover characteristic of Multi-AZ with the READABLE-standby characteristic more associated with read replicas, a genuinely real, purpose-built middle-ground option, not a redundant restatement of either classic Multi-AZ or standard read replicas alone.');

VALUES ('00783c98-7c36-44f6-a885-6c8accd2d7d5', '63368e78-2b0d-4abd-8a2f-6a1a58a1467e', 9, 'A company runs an Auto Scaling Group where new instances take several minutes to fully initialize (installing software, warming an in-memory cache) before they''re actually ready to serve traffic, which means a sudden scale-out event during a genuine traffic spike doesn''t actually help relieve load until those several minutes have passed. What ASG feature reduces this "ready to help but not yet" delay by keeping a pool of pre-initialized instances on standby, ready to be quickly moved into service?');
VALUES ('00783c98-7c36-44f6-a885-6c8accd2d7d5', 'b1bc9b93-697b-4afb-b676-45a31ffeca83', 'Warm Pools directly address the "launch-to-ready" delay gap by doing the time-consuming initialization work (software installation, cache warming, any other slow startup process) AHEAD of an actual scaling need, keeping those pre-initialized instances in a standby pool (which can itself be configured as stopped, for lower cost while waiting, or running, for the fastest possible activation) so that when a genuine scale-out event is triggered, the ASG can move an already-initialized instance into active service far faster than launching and fully initializing a brand-new one from scratch at that exact moment — directly relevant to genuinely sudden, unplanned traffic spikes where Scheduled Scaling (covered generally in a prior paper, which handles PREDICTABLE, known-in-advance patterns) wouldn''t help, since a scheduled scaling action only helps with traffic patterns known in advance. This is a genuinely different mechanism from ASG Lifecycle Hooks (covered generally in a prior paper, which pause an instance at a specific point in its launch or termination sequence to run custom actions, a related but distinct capability from maintaining a standing pre-warmed pool) — Warm Pools are a real, standard, purpose-built ASG feature specifically for reducing scale-out latency, not a capability ASGs lack.');

VALUES ('a7240e78-40be-4fb5-a2c3-7505f06ae1ee', '63368e78-2b0d-4abd-8a2f-6a1a58a1467e', 10, 'A company uses DynamoDB Global Tables (covered generally in a prior paper) with active-active replication across three Regions, and the SAME item is updated concurrently in two different Regions within the same brief window, before the two updates have had a chance to fully replicate to each other. Once replication completes, which update does DynamoDB keep, and what is this conflict-resolution approach called?');
VALUES ('a7240e78-40be-4fb5-a2c3-7505f06ae1ee', 'dce0c1d0-2996-4bfd-b130-26b5ba90aff5', 'Last writer wins is the specific, real conflict-resolution mechanism DynamoDB Global Tables use for exactly this scenario — since Global Tables use ACTIVE-ACTIVE replication (any Region can accept writes, covered generally in a prior paper), a genuinely real possibility exists that the same item gets updated in two different Regions before those two writes have had time to fully propagate to each other, and DynamoDB resolves this by keeping whichever write carries the later timestamp once both updates have propagated, with the earlier-timestamped write simply being overwritten/discarded rather than merged or manually reconciled. This is a genuinely important, directly testable architectural characteristic — an application design that assumes every single write is always durably preserved with no possibility of being silently superseded by a concurrent write from another Region needs to specifically account for this behavior (e.g., by designing data models or access patterns that minimize the realistic likelihood of the same specific item being concurrently modified from multiple Regions, or by being genuinely tolerant of last-writer-wins semantics for the specific use case). DynamoDB does NOT reject conflicting writes requiring manual resolution, does NOT perform automatic field-level merging, and concurrent cross-Region writes to the same item are a genuinely real possibility, not something the active-active architecture prevents outright — all three distractors describe behavior Global Tables do not actually have.');

VALUES ('5d2cb9f9-8a80-4b68-8764-343a13acf5c1', '63368e78-2b0d-4abd-8a2f-6a1a58a1467e', 11, 'A company using AWS Backup (covered generally in a prior paper) to centrally manage backups across EBS, RDS, and DynamoDB wants an ADDITIONAL guarantee that once a backup is created, it genuinely cannot be deleted or modified by anyone — including a compromised administrator credential — for a mandatory minimum retention period, similar in spirit to S3 Object Lock''s WORM protection but applied to the centralized backup vault itself. What AWS Backup feature provides this?');
VALUES ('5d2cb9f9-8a80-4b68-8764-343a13acf5c1', '5564632c-6487-498c-b565-5d6a551a63d0', 'Backup Vault Lock extends AWS Backup (covered generally in a prior paper, as the centralized cross-service backup management layer for EBS, RDS, DynamoDB, and others) with a genuinely important additional protection layer conceptually parallel to S3 Object Lock (covered in a prior paper, WORM protection for individual S3 OBJECTS) but applied instead to an entire BACKUP VAULT — once placed into compliance mode, NEITHER the account root user NOR any administrator, regardless of how broad their IAM permissions otherwise are, can delete backups in that vault or shorten their configured minimum retention period before that retention period has genuinely elapsed, directly closing a real gap that pure IAM-permission-based protection alone can''t fully close (since a sufficiently compromised or malicious credential with admin-level IAM permissions could otherwise simply delete or alter backup retention through normal API calls). This is a genuinely meaningful extra layer specifically protecting against insider threat or compromised-credential scenarios (both covered generally in Security+ content) targeting the backup data itself — ransomware-recovery scenarios specifically benefit from this, since an attacker who''s compromised administrative credentials as part of a broader attack often specifically attempts to delete or corrupt backups to eliminate the victim''s recovery option, which Vault Lock is directly designed to prevent. This is a real, standard AWS Backup feature, not a capability the service lacks.');

-- ── Domain: High-Performing Architectures (24% — 5 questions) ──

VALUES ('342d9f81-46a7-4459-a057-3c7386e28fd6', '63368e78-2b0d-4abd-8a2f-6a1a58a1467e', 12, 'A CloudFront distribution serves content globally, and AWS automatically routes a cache miss at a nearby edge location through an intermediate caching tier (with a larger cache and fewer total locations than the full edge network) BEFORE that request ever reaches the actual origin server — increasing the overall likelihood of a cache hit somewhere in AWS''s network without the origin needing to handle the request at all. What is this built-in intermediate CloudFront caching tier called?');
VALUES ('342d9f81-46a7-4459-a057-3c7386e28fd6', '14378a31-52de-4c5c-888a-055639274259', 'Regional Edge Caches are a genuinely distinct, AUTOMATIC (no opt-in or configuration required) intermediate tier from Origin Shield (covered in a prior paper, which is an explicitly OPT-IN, single additional caching layer a distribution owner specifically enables and selects a location for) — Regional Edge Caches sit between the full, globally-distributed set of individual edge locations and the origin, with fewer total locations than edge POPs but a meaningfully larger cache size at each, so a cache miss at a nearby edge location checks the Regional Edge Cache next (which, due to its larger capacity and the aggregation of traffic from multiple nearby edge locations, has a higher chance of already holding the requested content from a DIFFERENT nearby edge location''s earlier cache miss) before the request ever reaches the actual origin. This meaningfully reduces origin load and improves the overall cache-hit rate across CloudFront''s network as a whole, genuinely distinct from CloudFront origin groups (covered in a prior paper, which handle FAILOVER between two configured origins, an entirely different concern from caching hierarchy) — Regional Edge Caches are a real, standard, automatically-active part of CloudFront''s architecture, not a non-existent or manually-configured feature.');

VALUES ('0ba9ea67-1802-4bc4-b036-1bf15ef25b57', '63368e78-2b0d-4abd-8a2f-6a1a58a1467e', 13, 'A DynamoDB table experiences a sudden, temporary surge of traffic concentrated on a small number of specific partition keys (a "hot" set of items suddenly going viral), and the team wants to understand whether DynamoDB automatically adapts to redistribute throughput capacity toward those specific hot partitions, or whether this always requires manual intervention like redesigning the partition key. What DynamoDB capability addresses temporary, uneven traffic distribution like this?');
VALUES ('0ba9ea67-1802-4bc4-b036-1bf15ef25b57', '1a0b8d1f-fa9b-4516-bf40-4110d2209662', 'Adaptive capacity is DynamoDB''s automatic, built-in response specifically to uneven, hot-partition traffic patterns, directly extending the hot-partition CONCEPT covered generally in a prior paper (which focused on the underlying CAUSE — a poorly-distributed partition key — and the DESIGN-level fix) with the operational MITIGATION DynamoDB itself automatically applies in the meantime: continuously monitoring actual per-partition traffic and dynamically shifting available throughput capacity toward partitions currently experiencing disproportionate load, helping absorb genuinely temporary or less-predictable spikes (a specific item suddenly going viral) without requiring an immediate manual redesign. This is a genuinely important nuance — adaptive capacity is a real, automatic mitigation, but it doesn''t fully substitute for good partition key DESIGN for a SUSTAINED, predictable hot-partition pattern, where redesigning the key (the fix covered in the prior paper) remains the more fundamental, durable solution. DAX (covered in a prior paper) is a READ-caching layer, an entirely different mechanism from partition-level throughput redistribution, and Global Tables (covered in a prior paper) address MULTI-REGION replication, not intra-table partition load balancing — DynamoDB genuinely does provide this automatic capability, contrary to the last distractor.');

VALUES ('629aa1ce-64f7-46d6-9b64-8bfebfea4fd0', '63368e78-2b0d-4abd-8a2f-6a1a58a1467e', 14, 'An Application Load Balancer serves a single application with multiple distinct components: requests to `/api/*` should route to one target group (a backend API service), requests to `/images/*` should route to a different target group (a static image service), and requests arriving at `images.example.com` (a different hostname) should route to yet another target group entirely. What ALB feature supports routing to different target groups based on the request''s URL path or hostname like this?');
VALUES ('629aa1ce-64f7-46d6-9b64-8bfebfea4fd0', '19d56b2e-ecec-48fa-8a19-8fa8d01d825d', 'Path-based and host-based routing are core, defining ALB capabilities operating at Layer 7 (the application/HTTP layer), directly leveraging the ALB''s ability to actually inspect HTTP-level request content (URL path, Host header) that a Layer 4 load balancer fundamentally cannot see — genuinely distinct from an NLB (covered generally in a prior paper, specifically for raw TCP/UDP traffic that has no HTTP-level concept of a "path" or "Host header" to route on in the first place, since NLB operates below that layer). By defining listener rules that match on path patterns and/or hostnames, a single ALB can efficiently front multiple distinct backend services or even multiple genuinely separate applications sharing one load balancer, rather than requiring a dedicated ALB per component — a real, standard, and commonly used architecture pattern for consolidating load balancing infrastructure while still cleanly separating traffic to the correct backend target group. CloudFront origin groups (covered in a prior paper) handle origin FAILOVER at the CDN layer, an entirely different concern from ALB-level request routing to different target groups — ALB fully supports this path/host-based routing as a core, standard, and well-documented feature, not a capability it lacks.');

VALUES ('213d181f-b6e8-4465-a517-8dc4f869af98', '63368e78-2b0d-4abd-8a2f-6a1a58a1467e', 15, 'A memory-intensive EC2 workload takes a long time to warm up its large in-memory application cache after a restart. The team wants to be able to fully pause the instance (stopping compute/storage billing for that instance''s compute time) between periods of use, but resume it with the RAM contents — including that warmed cache — fully intact, rather than needing to rebuild that in-memory state from scratch on every restart. What EC2 feature supports this?');
VALUES ('213d181f-b6e8-4465-a517-8dc4f869af98', '7b0e8026-85a3-422e-b2c5-fd03bd967411', 'EC2 Hibernate specifically addresses the "stop billing for compute time, but don''t lose the warmed-up in-memory state" need by saving the instance''s actual RAM contents to its root EBS volume at the moment of stopping, then restoring that exact saved memory state when the instance starts again — genuinely different from a STANDARD EC2 stop/start (which does NOT preserve RAM contents at all; a standard restart is a normal cold boot, meaning any in-memory cache or application state genuinely has to be rebuilt from scratch, taking real time for a workload with a slow warm-up process). This is directly useful for exactly the described scenario — an application with an expensive-to-rebuild in-memory cache that would otherwise need to fully re-warm after every single restart — letting the team stop paying for that instance''s compute time between periods of actual use, without sacrificing the time investment already made warming up its in-memory state. EC2 auto-recovery (covered generally in a prior paper) is a different capability entirely — automatically recovering an instance from a detected underlying HARDWARE failure, not preserving RAM across a deliberate, planned stop/start cycle — this is a real, standard EC2 feature (with some real prerequisites and instance-type/size constraints), not a capability that doesn''t exist.');

-- ── Domain: Cost-Optimized Architectures (20% — 4 questions) ──

VALUES ('bcb4249c-0e51-4320-9110-a2ea2f506bf3', '63368e78-2b0d-4abd-8a2f-6a1a58a1467e', 16, 'A company under AWS Organizations'' Consolidated Billing (covered generally in a prior paper) has a Reserved Instance purchased in one member account, but a DIFFERENT member account is now running matching, unused EC2 instance usage. Does the RI''s billing discount automatically apply across accounts within the same Organization, or is it strictly confined to the purchasing account?');
VALUES ('bcb4249c-0e51-4320-9110-a2ea2f506bf3', '6ee6b72f-d4a6-4347-a6c5-957750820155', 'This extends the Consolidated Billing concept (covered generally in a prior paper, for aggregated volume-pricing benefits across an Organization) with a genuinely important, specific benefit directly relevant to RI (covered generally in a prior paper, including the Standard-vs-Convertible distinction) and Savings Plans (also covered generally) purchasing strategy: by default, an RI or Savings Plan''s discount benefit is NOT confined to only the specific member account that made the purchase — it automatically applies across every member account in the Organization to any MATCHING, otherwise-undiscounted usage, meaning a central team could deliberately purchase RIs/Savings Plans covering the Organization''s AGGREGATE, predictable baseline usage without needing to know in advance exactly which individual member account will actually run that matching workload, since the discount finds and applies to matching usage wherever it occurs across the Organization. This default sharing behavior CAN be selectively turned off per account for organizations that specifically want cost/usage isolation between accounts, but the DEFAULT is sharing, not isolation — no physical instance transfer or additional service subscription is required for this to work; it''s a standard, built-in characteristic of purchasing RIs/Savings Plans under Organizations Consolidated Billing.');

VALUES ('5cea947d-ede8-4c57-b4cd-59dc839b2167', '63368e78-2b0d-4abd-8a2f-6a1a58a1467e', 17, 'A company has a critical, time-sensitive event (a major product launch) where they need an absolute GUARANTEE that a specific quantity and type of EC2 capacity will genuinely be available in a specific Availability Zone at that exact time, but the actual usage will only last a few days around the event — making a 1- or 3-year Reserved Instance commitment inappropriate for capacity they only need briefly. What EC2 mechanism provides a capacity GUARANTEE without requiring a long-term RI-style commitment?');
VALUES ('5cea947d-ede8-4c57-b4cd-59dc839b2167', '3090ac8a-57b2-46c9-a3ab-dab36cbc8256', 'On-Demand Capacity Reservations specifically decouple the two things RIs (covered generally in a prior paper) normally bundle together — a capacity GUARANTEE and a billing DISCOUNT — offering ONLY the capacity guarantee, with NO long-term commitment requirement and NO billing discount of its own (usage is simply billed at standard On-Demand rates for as long as the reservation is held), making ODCR specifically well suited to a genuinely short-term but capacity-critical need like a specific product launch event, where a 1- or 3-year RI commitment would be entirely the wrong fit for capacity only actually needed for a few days. This is a genuinely useful, real, and directly testable distinction — ODCRs CAN also be combined with RIs or Savings Plans for a workload that needs both the capacity guarantee AND a cost discount over a longer period, but as a standalone mechanism, ODCR''s specific value is the CAPACITY assurance alone, independent of commitment length. A Spot Instance (covered generally in a prior paper) is the functional OPPOSITE of a capacity guarantee — Spot capacity can be reclaimed by AWS with short notice, making it specifically unsuitable for a "must be available with certainty" requirement — and a Standard RI inherently requires the 1- or 3-year commitment this scenario specifically needs to avoid.');

VALUES ('29bc305f-4b34-4346-9268-a72ca1e4b1ba', '63368e78-2b0d-4abd-8a2f-6a1a58a1467e', 18, 'A company self-manages a NAT instance (a regular EC2 instance configured to perform network address translation for a private subnet) instead of using a managed NAT Gateway, specifically to save on the NAT Gateway''s hourly and per-GB data processing charges. What are the real operational trade-offs of this choice compared to a managed NAT Gateway?');
VALUES ('29bc305f-4b34-4346-9268-a72ca1e4b1ba', 'ffef9c4f-744d-4d8e-ab11-14d9cc7ca222', 'This directly extends the NAT Gateway cost concept (covered generally in a prior paper, including its hourly-plus-per-GB pricing structure and the Gateway VPC Endpoint pattern for avoiding some of that cost for specific AWS-service-bound traffic) with the real, standard alternative and its genuine trade-offs: a self-managed NAT instance is simply a regular EC2 instance running NAT software, billed as ordinary EC2 compute rather than NAT Gateway''s specific pricing model, which CAN be meaningfully cheaper for low, steady traffic volumes — but that cost saving comes with real, non-trivial operational responsibility shifted onto the company itself, since AWS''s NAT Gateway is FULLY MANAGED (no patching, no availability management, no manual scaling needed from the customer), while a self-managed NAT instance requires the team to handle all of that themselves, represents a genuine single point of failure/bottleneck unless the team separately builds additional HA architecture around it (e.g., a standby instance with automated failover), and generally cannot match a NAT Gateway''s ability to automatically scale to very high bandwidth. This is a real, standard, and genuinely relevant cost-versus-operational-burden trade-off decision architects actually make, not a case where either option is strictly and unconditionally superior in every dimension, or where the NAT instance alternative doesn''t genuinely exist as a real, available option.');

VALUES ('e570c979-f951-4677-9263-d39049321e94', '63368e78-2b0d-4abd-8a2f-6a1a58a1467e', 19, 'A company runs frequent S3 multipart uploads (covered generally in a prior paper) for large files, but some of those uploads fail partway through (a network interruption, an application crash) and are never explicitly completed or aborted — leaving behind incomplete upload parts that continue silently accumulating S3 storage charges indefinitely, since an incomplete multipart upload''s parts are still stored data even though the overall upload never finished. What S3 feature automatically cleans up these abandoned incomplete uploads after a configurable period?');
VALUES ('e570c979-f951-4677-9263-d39049321e94', 'efbfe7b8-0662-4594-8f96-6a8c20bccc51', 'This extends the general S3 Lifecycle concept (covered generally in prior papers, for transitioning objects between storage classes) with a specific, genuinely easy-to-overlook rule type: targeting INCOMPLETE MULTIPART UPLOADS specifically (rather than completed objects) for automatic cleanup — since a multipart upload (covered generally in a prior paper, for large-file uploads split into parts) that fails or is abandoned partway through leaves its already-uploaded PARTS sitting in S3 as real, billed storage even though no complete, usable object was ever actually finalized, and without an explicit lifecycle rule targeting this specific case, those abandoned parts continue accumulating storage cost indefinitely with no automatic expiration by default. Configuring a lifecycle rule to automatically abort (and thereby delete the stored parts of) any multipart upload not completed within a defined number of days directly closes this real, practical cost leak — a genuinely common real-world S3 cost-optimization finding, precisely because it''s the kind of accumulating cost that''s easy to overlook entirely (unlike a large, obviously-visible completed object) unless someone specifically knows to look for and configure this. S3 Versioning (covered generally in a prior paper) and Storage Class Analysis (covered in a prior paper) are both genuinely unrelated features to incomplete-upload cleanup, and incomplete multipart uploads are NOT automatically deleted by S3 with no configuration — this requires an explicitly configured lifecycle rule.');

-- ── Domain: High-Performing Architectures (additional — question 20) ──

VALUES ('6126ae79-0c2e-4ca1-a008-6eaa18d9a42d', '63368e78-2b0d-4abd-8a2f-6a1a58a1467e', 20, 'An Aurora database runs a complex analytical query that scans and aggregates a very large number of rows (e.g., a large SUM or COUNT across millions of rows), and this single query is taking a long time to complete because it''s being processed sequentially by the database engine''s compute layer alone. What Aurora feature can meaningfully speed up this kind of large-scan analytical query by pushing part of the processing down closer to the underlying storage layer, executed in parallel?');
VALUES ('6126ae79-0c2e-4ca1-a008-6eaa18d9a42d', '2aeda7ff-a76f-4be9-a31b-8b7e8cd644b9', 'Aurora Parallel Query addresses a genuinely different performance dimension from read SCALING (the general Read Replica concept, covered in a prior paper, which scales read CAPACITY by adding more instances each independently serving separate queries) — Parallel Query instead speeds up a SINGLE large, scan-heavy analytical query by pushing filtering and aggregation work down into Aurora''s distributed storage layer, which processes that work in parallel across many storage nodes simultaneously, rather than the traditional approach of pulling all the relevant raw data up to the single compute instance and processing it there sequentially. This is specifically well suited to genuinely large-scan, aggregation-heavy analytical queries (a large SUM/COUNT/aggregation across millions of rows) where the bottleneck is the sheer volume of data that needs to be scanned and filtered, not simply a need for more concurrent read capacity across many separate queries — a meaningfully different performance problem than what adding more read replicas addresses. This is a genuinely distinct Aurora feature from Aurora Backtrack (covered in a prior paper, a point-in-time REWIND/recovery capability) and Aurora Global Database (covered in a prior paper, CROSS-REGION replication for global read latency) — three separate, purpose-built Aurora capabilities, each solving a different problem, and Parallel Query is a real, standard feature genuinely going beyond what read replicas alone provide for this specific analytical-query use case.');
